---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../../data/standards/sic/sic32.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType="IFRS"
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Definitions" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Research/Development Split" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Planning Stage Costs" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Development Stage Costs" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Recognition Criteria" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Amortisation & Impairment" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Disclosure" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s10", label: "SIC-32 vs IFRS" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why SIC-32 exists as a bridge interpretation</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 38 applies the same recognition criteria to ALL internally generated intangible assets, including those created through website development. However, website development projects span multiple stages – from initial planning and feasibility, through design and development, to ongoing maintenance and upgrades. SIC-32 clarifies how IAS 38's recognition criteria are applied across these stages, establishing that only the DEVELOPMENT stage generates an intangible asset; planning stage costs are expensed.</div>
        <div class="note">Without SIC-32, entities developing their own e-commerce platforms or business websites could have capitalised ALL costs from the outset – including feasibility studies, branding assessments, and planning contingencies – as an intangible asset, over-stating assets and under-stating early-period expenses.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Applying the IAS 38 research/development split to websites</div>
        <div class="principle">SIC-32 maps the two-stage taxonomy of IAS 38 (research → development) onto the web development lifecycle. Planning and feasibility work is RESEARCH – it does not meet the recognition criteria and must be expensed. Once the entity has moved into the actual construction and construction-ready phase, the project enters DEVELOPMENT stage, and IAS 38's recognition criteria begin to apply.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope & Definitions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What SIC-32 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">SIC-32 applies to costs incurred in the development of a website BY the entity FOR its OWN use – not for sale to customers (which falls under IAS 2, Inventories). The site must be an internally generated intangible asset. Where a website is developed specifically for sale, it is inventory; where it is for the entity's own use, it is an intangible asset under IAS 38.</div>
        <div class="elabel">TYPICAL FACT PATTERNS</span>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>An e-commerce platform developed in-house to support the entity's own distribution operations</span>
          <span>A customer portal built for the entity's own use to service clients via self-service</span>
          <span>An internal resource hub created to support employee training and knowledge management</span>
          <span>An intranet site developed to replace an internal procurement system</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Website (internally generated)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">An internally generated intangible asset created through the development of a website for the entity's own use – subject to IAS 38's recognition criteria and split into planning (research) and development stages per SIC-32.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Software application</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The underlying code, middleware, and database infrastructure that enables the website to function – treated separately from graphic content under IAS 38.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Research/Development Split</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The two-stage lifecycle of a website</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-1-1-1-2"><span>Stage</span><span>IAS 38 label</span><span>Outcome</span><span>Accounting treatment</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-1-1-1-2"><span>Planning stage</span><span>Research</span><span>Feasibility assessment; choices between CMS, custom code, or third-party platform; graphic design decisions</span><span>Expensed as incurred – cannot be capitalised under IAS 38</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-1-1-1-2"><span>Development stage</span><span>Development</span><span>Construction of pages; coding functionality; integration with back-end systems; testing and QA</span><span>Capitalised when, and only when, all six IAS 38 recognition criteria are met</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">SIC-32 is explicit that the boundary between the two stages is NOT at the point of coding. Progress beyond planning does NOT automatically trigger capitalisation. Only when an entity can demonstrate ALL six IAS 38 recognition criteria (technical feasibility, intention to complete, ability to use or sell, how it will generate future economic benefits, availability of resources, and ability to measure the expenditure reliably) does capitalisation commence.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Planning Stage Costs</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What must be expensed</div>
        <div class="principle">All costs incurred in the PLANNING stage are expensed as incurred. This includes:</div>
        <div class="elabel">EXPENSED COSTS</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Feasibility studies for the website development</span>
          <span>Preliminary planning and evaluation of alternatives for the website</span>
          <span>Research (e.g. searching for the appropriate technology, evaluating different web approaches)</span>
          <span>Graphic design decisions that do not yet meet the recognition criteria</span>
          <span>Administrative overheads and other indirect costs attributable to the planning phase</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush">The planning stage parallels IAS 38's "research" phase – nothing is certain enough about the entity's ability to complete and use the website to justify capitalisation.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key principle</div>
        <div class="principle">Expense recognition is the DEFAULT. There is a strong presumption against capitalising costs without clear evidence of each IAS 38 recognition criterion being met. Entities must document why planning costs do not meet those criteria – the burden of proof is on capitalisation, not on expensing.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Development Stage Costs</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">What can be capitalised</div>
        <div class="principle">Once the entity has moved into the DEVELOPMENT stage and ALL six IAS 38 recognition criteria are met, costs can be capitalised. This typically includes:</div>
        <div class="elabel">CAPITALISABLE COSTS</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Costs of materials and services consumed in developing or obtaining access to current technologies</span>
          <span>Costs to create the website's software application directly attributable to bringing the asset to working condition</span>
          <span>Direct labour costs of employees engaged in development</span>
          <span>Payments to third-party developers for bespoke functionality</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The boundary between development and maintenance</div>
        <div class="principle">Ongoing maintenance – even if it enhances the website's functionality – is expensed NOT capitalised. Once the website is available for use, costs incurred to maintain or upgrade it are treated as repairs and maintenance, not as development costs, UNLESS they meet the recognition criteria as SEPARATE intangible assets and can be distinguished from the existing asset.</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: Capitalisation vs. maintenance</div>
          <div class="wk-body">An entity's website was capitalised at €500,000. In year 2, the entity spends €80,000 upgrading the checkout system to handle multi-currency payments. The upgrade extends functionality previously unavailable and provides future economic benefits.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">If the upgrade costs meet IAS 38's recognition criteria – specifically technical feasibility, ability to complete, and probable future economic benefit – then the €80,000 is capitalised as a separate enhancement asset. If the upgrade is merely a security patch or cosmetic refresh, it must be expensed.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Recognition Criteria</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">All six IAS 38 criteria must be met</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-1-2"><span>Criterion</span><span>Application to website projects</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-1-2"><span>Technical feasibility</span><span>The entity can demonstrate that an intangible asset capable of generating future economic benefits exists – the website can be completed and made operational</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-1-2"><span>Intention to complete</span><span>Management has demonstrated the intention to complete the website and use it for its intended purpose</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-1-2"><span>Ability to use or sell</span><span>The website can be used internally or sold externally – for own-use sites, this means the entity can operate it to serve its needs</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-1-2"><span>How it will generate future economic benefits</span><span>The entity can demonstrate the mechanism – e.g. increased sales, reduced costs, improved customer service – through which the website will contribute to future cash flows</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-1-2"><span>Availability of resources</span><span>The entity has the technical, financial, and other resources to complete development and use the site</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-1-2"><span>Ability to measure expenditure reliably</span><span>The costs of the development stage can be separately identified and measured – a critical criterion given the complexity of web development projects</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">Meeting ALL six criteria is a high bar. If ANY criterion is not met at a particular point, costs must continue to be expensed until all criteria are demonstrably satisfied. SIC-32 does not create a lower threshold for websites than IAS 38's general standard.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Amortisation & Impairment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Post-recognition treatment</div>
        <div class="principle">Once a website is recognised as an intangible asset, it is amortised over its useful economic life on a systematic basis. The useful life of a website is typically short – often 3-5 years – because technology evolves rapidly. However, each entity assesses the useful life based on its own expected usage and technological obsolescence.</div>
        <div class="elabel">SIC-32 CONFIRMS</span>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Amortisation method (e.g. straight-line, units of production) should reflect the consumption pattern of the website's economic benefits</span>
          <span>IAS 36 impairment testing applies – the carrying amount of the website must not exceed its recoverable amount</span>
          <span>If the website is expected to generate benefits only for a limited period, the useful life should reflect that period</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Technological obsolescence</div>
        <div class="principle">Website-specific risks – such as browser incompatibility, security vulnerabilities, and changing user expectations – can cause a website to become obsolete more rapidly than a traditional software asset. Entities should assess indicators of impairment at each reporting date in line with IAS 36, including changes in the technological environment that could diminish the website's recoverable amount.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">What SIC-32 / IAS 38 requires</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Whether the website is carried at cost or revalued amount (revaluation basis is permitted under IAS 38 for some intangible assets, though uncommon for websites)</span>
          <span>The gross carrying amount and accumulated amortisation at the beginning and end of the period</span>
          <span>Amortisation method, useful life, and amortisation charge for the period</span>
          <span>Gross amount of any additions, disposals, and impairment losses recognised in the period</span>
          <span>Reconciliation of the carrying amount from opening to closing balance</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>The boundary between planning stage and development stage – a subjective call that affects the size of capitalised vs. expensed amounts</span>
              <span>Whether an enhancement to an existing website meets IAS 38 recognition criteria as a separate asset</span>
              <span>Whether the website is an intangible asset for own use (IAS 38) or inventory (IAS 2)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Useful economic life of the website in years – materially affects amortisation charge and asset carrying amount</span>
              <span>Remaining useful life at each reporting date for impairment testing</span>
              <span>Allocation of costs between planning expensed and capitalised development stages</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>SIC-32 vs IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – SIC-32 within the broader IFRS framework</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-1-1-1"><span>Aspect</span><span>SIC-32 (IFRS)</span><span>US GAAP (ASC 350)</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-1-1-1"><span>Planning stage costs</span><span>Expensed – cannot be capitalised as development</span><span>Generally similar – planning and preliminary design costs expensed</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-1-1-1"><span>Development stage costs</span><span>Capitalised IF AND ONLY IF all six IAS 38 recognition criteria are met</span><span>Similar criteria under ASC 350; some differences in application specifics</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-1-1-1"><span>Post-completion upgrades</span><span>Expensed unless they meet recognition criteria as separate assets</span><span>Similar treatment – maintenance costs expensed; only enhancements with separately identifiable future benefits capitalised</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Where every SIC-32 event lands</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Planning stage costs</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Expensed as incurred – no asset recognition</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Development stage costs – meeting IAS 38 criteria</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Capitalised as intangible asset; amortised over useful life</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Website available for use – amortisation begins</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Amortisation over useful life from the date it is available for use</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Post-completion upgrades</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Capitalised ONLY if they meet IAS 38 recognition criteria as a separate asset</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Impairment loss recognised</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>IAS 36 impairment testing applied; loss recognised in profit or loss</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 38</span><span>The parent standard SIC-32 applies directly to website development costs, including the research/development split and general recognition criteria</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IAS 16</span><span>Governs the hardware (servers, network equipment) used to host and operate the website – outside SIC-32's scope</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 2</span><span>Applies instead where a website is developed for SALE to a customer, rather than for the entity's own use</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Internally generated intangible asset</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">An intangible asset created by the entity itself through development activities rather than acquired from an external party – subject to specific recognition criteria under IAS 38, refined by SIC-32 for websites.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Research stage</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The phase of web development before criteria for capitalisation are met – costs are expensed. SIC-32 identifies this as the planning stage for website projects.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Website (internally generated)", definition: "An internally generated intangible asset created through the development of a website for the entity's own use, subject to IAS 38's recognition criteria and SIC-32's research/development split." },
      { term: "Planning stage costs", definition: "Costs incurred in the research/planning phase of website development – feasibility studies, design exploration, and evaluation work that must be expensed under IAS 38/SIC-32." },
      { term: "Development stage costs", definition: "Costs incurred after planning when all IAS 38 recognition criteria are met – coding, integration, and testing costs that meet the capitalisation threshold." },
      { term: "Technological obsolescence", definition: "The risk that a website becomes outdated or functionally impaired due to changes in technology – a key impairment indicator under IAS 36 for web-based intangible assets." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.SIC32} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>




